<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Most owners who tell me “the website isn’t working” are looking at the wrong problem. Traffic is there: people search, click and land. Then they leave. They didn’t hate the site. It just never gave them a reason to act today, with you, rather than the next result on the page.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If this sounds familiar, the fix is usually not a new logo or a new template. It’s the way the site is built to sell — which is what I mean when I talk about <a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/services/website-design-development/' ) ); ?>">treating a website as a digital sales system</a>. Here are the five places interested visitors most often give up.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">1. The first screen answers the wrong question</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Most homepages open with who the company is: “Family-owned since…”, “Quality craftsmanship…”. A visitor who just searched “kitchen remodeler Fort Myers” is asking something else: <strong>can you do my job, in my area, at the level I want?</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The first screen should answer that in one sentence, name the area you serve and show one clear next step. If a visitor has to scroll to find out what you do, many won’t.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">2. Proof is hidden where nobody looks</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Reviews live on a separate page. Project photos sit in a gallery with no context. Yet proof matters most at the moment of doubt: next to the price question, next to the contact form, right under the headline.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Put one strong project and one real review on every page that asks for a call. A named customer from a nearby city outweighs ten anonymous five-star quotes.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">3. Every service shares one page</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If your pool resurfacing, new builds and repairs share a single “Services” page, you’re invisible for most of the searches your customers actually make — and the visitors who do arrive have to dig for the part that matters to them.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Each high-value service deserves its own page: what it includes, who it’s for, a relevant project and a way to ask about it. That page is both what ranks and what converts.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">4. Contacting you takes work</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Many local customers decide on a phone, often in between other things. Test your own site on a phone:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Is the phone number tappable from every page, without scrolling?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Does the contact form fit on one screen, with only the fields you really need?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Does the visitor know what happens after they submit — and how fast?</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Each extra step loses people who were ready to talk.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">5. Nobody knows which visits turn into jobs</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Without call tracking and form tracking, you can’t tell which pages and searches bring customers, so every change is a guess. The goal isn’t more traffic reports. It’s one number you trust: <strong>qualified opportunities per month</strong>, and where they came from.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Where to start</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Fix them in this order: the first screen, proof placement, contact on mobile, then service pages, then tracking. The first three are often a few days of work and can change results within weeks. Service pages and tracking take longer, and they are also what <a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/services/local-seo/' ) ); ?>">local SEO</a> builds on.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you’d like a second pair of eyes, the <a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/growth-scorecard/' ) ); ?>">Digital Growth Scorecard</a> takes five minutes and shows which of these areas is holding you back most.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
